# SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000154  -  CAT II
# Cookies exchanged between the web server and the client, such as session
# cookies, must have cookie properties set to prohibit client-side scripts
# from reading the cookie data.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000154
group:           V-206437
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-002418
control_rev5:    SC-8

contribution:    Splunk can mark both the Splunk Web session cookie and the splunkd
                 authentication cookie HttpOnly so that running JavaScript cannot read
                 their values.

governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.httponly in 1|true
check:           server.conf [httpServer] cookieAuthHttpOnly in 1|true

external:        Browser enforcement of the HttpOnly attribute
external:        Any installed app or dashboard JavaScript that creates its own cookies,
                 which is outside these platform settings

citation:        SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000154 check text; web.conf.spec:943-950;
                 server.conf.spec:1520-1531
finding:         Splunk returns session or authentication cookies without the HttpOnly
                 attribute (web.conf tools.sessions.httponly or server.conf
                 cookieAuthHttpOnly is false), so client side scripts can read the cookie
                 value.
